==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fro|desagraable}} (compare {{cog|fr|désagréable}}). Surface etymology is {{prefix|en|dis|agreeable}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|[dɪsəˈɡɹi.əbəɫ]}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-disagreeable.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Causing [[repugnance]]; [[unpleasant]] to the feelings or senses; [[displeasing]].
#: {{ux|en|'''disagreeable''' weather}}
#: {{ux|en|'''disagreeable''' person}}
#: {{ux|en|'''disagreeable''' attitude}}
#: {{ux|en|'''disagreeable''' taste}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1722|author=William Wollaston|title=The Religion of Nature Delineated|passage=That which is '''disagreeable''' to one is many times agreeable to another, or '''disagreeable''' in a less degree.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Not [[suitable]]; that does not [[conform]] or [[fit]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1738| title=A Supplement to the Sermons Lately Preached at Salters-Hall Against Popery|author=Grantham Killingworth | page=54| passage=The first author I shall cite is Justin Martyr, who is not only silent about infant-baptism, as are all the fathers before him, but says what is inconsistent with it; for the reason he assigns, as what he had received from the apostles themselves, why baptism was instituted, is altogether incompatible and '''disagreeable''' thereunto.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1824| title=Gospel Advocate - Volume 1 |author=| page=140| passage=Now to say, that justice is opposed to forgiveness, when by forgiveness we mean the entire cure of sin and misery; is to say that justice chooses that to remain forever, which is perfectly '''disagreeable''' to itself.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1871| title=A History of New England with Particular Reference to the Denomination of Christians Called Baptists|author=Isaac Backus | page=466| passage=We cannot but judge it '''disagreeable''' with the spirit and principles of the gospel, and an encroachment upon the divine prerogative and the undoubted rights of mankind, to punish any for their conscientious and peaceable dissent from the established way of religion, while they are not justly chargeable with any immorality, or what is plainly destructive of civil society.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|a. 1668| title=The Maritime Dicaeologie, Or, Sea-jurisdiction of England|author=John Exton | page=55| passage=and that this Nation that is invested in a Law particular and municipal, which doth in no wise so much as challenge or claim any derivation from, or dependency of the Civil Law, but is altogether different and '''disagreeable''' thereunto in its proceedings, and oftentimes in its determinations, and hath no foundation or grounds whereon the decisions of Sea controversies can be built, should without the knowledge and practise of these Civil and Maritime Laws deal and trade with such as do live under them, and are guided by them?}}

